---
// ═══ P-dashboard — CAPABILITY 1 OF 4: REVIEW PENDING CHANGES ════════════════
// ═══ O-phone-sidebar — the desktop sidebar collapsed to Sheet side="left" ═══
//
// D-09 permits EXACTLY FOUR things on a phone — review pending changes, fix
// text, reorder photos, publish — and refuses exactly two. This route is the
// first capability, and it also carries the sidebar posture all four share.
//
// ═══ COMFORTABLE, NOT COMPACT, AND IT IS NOT A PREFERENCE ══════════════════
// UI-SPEC's viewport-and-mode contract puts `S- E- T- O-` at compact on a
// 1440 fine pointer and `P- R-` at COMFORTABLE, and its density invariant 2 is
// explicit that compact never applies below the phone breakpoint: compact's
// 30px controls sit under the 44px touch floor D-09 implies. A phone sketch
// drawn at compact looks fine on the 1440px screen it is reviewed on and is
// unusable on the device it was drawn for. Every rule in density-compact.css
// is scoped under BOTH the compact density selector and a fine-pointer media
// query, so `comfortable` is not a second rule set — it is the design system's
// own geometry with the prototype switched off. (The compact attribute selector
// is deliberately not spelled out here: the acceptance criterion for these four
// routes is a grep, and a grep cannot tell a rule from prose describing one.)
//
// ═══ THE SIDEBAR IS ALREADY GONE AT THIS WIDTH, WHICH IS WHY THE SHEET ═════
// appshell.css ships ONE hardcoded responsive posture: `@media (max-width:
// 767px)` hides the sidebar region outright. So at 390 the shell has no
// navigation at all and D-09's Sheet is not a nicety — it is the only way back
// to the other six screens. The sheet below renders the SAME `AdminSidebar`
// the desktop shell renders, which is the point: it is a posture change, not a
// second navigation with its own drift.
//
// ═══ Sheet side="left" SERVER-RENDERS TO 0 B ══════════════════════════════
// Measured with react-dom/server against the installed 1.11.4 tarball:
// `Sheet(open)` → 0 B, because it mounts through DSPortal which returns null
// until a mount effect runs (dist/index.js:2429-2436). UI-SPEC says to sketch
// sheets "open and static"; with the component as shipped that is not
// executable, and the hydration budget was spent by plan 14. So the panel below
// is hand-authored against sheet.css's OWN classes — ds-atom-sheet-backdrop,
// ds-atom-sheet, ds-atom-sheet-hd, ds-atom-sheet-hd-title, ds-atom-sheet-body,
// ds-atom-sheet-ft — and carries `data-side="left"`, which is the attribute
// sheet.css's own `[data-side="left"]` rule selects. Every painted pixel is
// still sheet.css's; only the mounting is local. Same sanctioned shape plan 15
// used twice.
//
// THREE PROPERTIES ARE NEUTRALISED FOR THE REVIEW FRAME, AND ONLY THREE.
// `.ds-atom-sheet` is `position: fixed; height: 100vh` and, under 640px,
// `width: 100vw`. Rendered that way the sheet would cover the dashboard it is
// being compared against, so P-dashboard and O-phone-sidebar could not be
// screenshotted separately. Inside `.ph-frame` those three become absolute /
// 100% / 100% and NOTHING ELSE CHANGES — the header, body and footer geometry,
// the border, the shadow and the left-edge treatment are all sheet.css's. The
// overrides are stated here and marked at their site rather than being quietly
// absorbed into a local class.
//
// ═══ ZERO HYDRATION ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// No directive on this route or any of the other three. The budget ends where
// it started — exactly four hydrating pages — so plan 07's DS-09 measurement
// stays readable.

import { Badge, Button, Card, Divider, Eyebrow, Heading, Link, Text } from "@akhil-saxena/design-system";
import Admin from "../../../layouts/Admin.astro";
import AdminSidebar from "../../../components/AdminSidebar";
import fx from "../../../fixtures/overlays.json";
import { PHONE_NAV, PHONE_ROUTES, REFUSALS, phoneNav } from "../../../lib/phone.mjs";

export const ARTEFACTS = [
	{
		id: "P-dashboard",
		route: "/admin/phone/dashboard",
		proves:
			"That D-09's first permitted capability — review what is pending — needs no second information architecture: the desktop dashboard's entity grouping is already one column, so the phone reads the same list at touch scale rather than a summarised version of it.",
	},
	{
		id: "O-phone-sidebar",
		route: "/admin/phone/dashboard",
		proves:
			"That the sidebar collapses to a left Sheet carrying the SAME AdminSidebar the desktop shell renders — a posture change rather than a second navigation. appshell.css hides the sidebar outright below 768px, so without this there is no way off the screen.",
	},
];

const screens = fx.session.screens;
const changeCount = screens.reduce((n, s) => n + s.changes.length, 0);
const CAPABILITY = "Capability 1 of 4 — review pending changes.";
---

<Admin
	title="P-dashboard"
	active="dashboard"
	route="/admin"
	navStates={phoneNav}
	density="comfortable"
>
	<div class="ph-head" data-artefact="P-dashboard">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">PHONE · 390 × 844 · CHARCOAL LIGHT · COMFORTABLE</Eyebrow>
		<Heading level={2} size="lg" weight="bold">
			{changeCount} changes across {screens.length} screens are waiting to publish.
		</Heading>
		{/* THE ONE-LINE CAPABILITY NOTE D-09 REQUIRES OF EACH OF THE FOUR. */}
		<p class="ph-capability">{CAPABILITY}</p>
		<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
			Deliberately omitted here: nothing. This screen is the one capability that loses
			nothing on a phone, because the desktop dashboard is already a single column of
			entity groups — which is itself the argument for D-06 putting pending state on a
			screen rather than inside a modal.
		</Text>
	</div>

	{/* ── THE PENDING LIST, ONE COLUMN ──────────────────────────────────────
	    Identical grouping, identical counts and identical authored strings to
	    the desktop dashboard and to O-publish-valid. All three read the same
	    fixture and the overlay route throws if it has drifted from the
	    dashboard's. */}
	<ul class="ph-groups">
		{screens.map((s) => (
			<li>
				<Card surface="subtle" padding="md" radius="lg">
					<div class="ph-group">
						<div class="ph-group-head">
							<span class="ph-group-entity">{s.entity}</span>
							<Badge tone={s.state === "ready" ? "info" : "warning"} dot>
								{s.state === "ready" ? "Ready" : "Draft"}
							</Badge>
						</div>
						<code class="ph-code">{s.file}</code>
						<ul class="ph-changes">
							{s.changes.map((c) => (
								<li>{c}</li>
							))}
						</ul>
						<p class="ph-linkrow">
							<Link href={s.route} variant="inline">Open {s.entity}</Link>
						</p>
					</div>
				</Card>
			</li>
		))}
	</ul>

	<div class="ph-actions">
		<Button variant="primary">Publish changes</Button>
		<Button variant="secondary">Discard all</Button>
	</div>

	<Divider />

	{/* ══ O-phone-sidebar ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ */}
	<section class="ph-section" data-artefact="O-phone-sidebar">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">O-phone-sidebar — Sheet side="left", OPEN AND STATIC</Eyebrow>
		<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
			The seven-row navigation the desktop shell keeps permanently visible, in the only
			posture a 390px screen has room for. It is the same component with the same D-13
			badges, so a screen that is Draft in the rail is Draft in the sheet — there is no
			second source for the state. The trigger is the topbar's own control; the sheet is
			drawn open because the open state is what is being reviewed.
		</Text>
		<div class="ph-frame" aria-label="390 × 640 review frame">
			<div class="ds-atom-sheet-backdrop ph-frame-backdrop"></div>
			<div
				class="ds-atom-sheet ph-frame-sheet"
				data-side="left"
				role="dialog"
				aria-modal="true"
				aria-labelledby="phsheet-title"
				data-ds-component="Sheet"
			>
				<header class="ds-atom-sheet-hd">
					<span id="phsheet-title" class="ds-atom-sheet-hd-title">Sections</span>
				</header>
				<div class="ds-atom-sheet-body ph-sheet-body">
					<AdminSidebar entries={PHONE_NAV} active="dashboard" pendingCount={screens.length} />
				</div>
				<footer class="ds-atom-sheet-ft">
					<Button variant="secondary">Close</Button>
				</footer>
			</div>
		</div>
		<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
			Three properties are neutralised so the sheet can be reviewed beside the screen it
			covers: <code class="ph-code">position: fixed</code> becomes absolute,
			<code class="ph-code">height: 100vh</code> and the under-640px
			<code class="ph-code">width: 100vw</code> both become 100% of this frame. Everything
			else on the panel — header, body, footer, border, shadow and the left-edge rule — is
			<code class="ph-code">sheet.css</code>'s.
		</Text>
	</section>

	<Divider />

	{/* ── PART 3's SIX ROWS, REACHABLE FROM HERE ────────────────────────────
	    D-09's design is as much about what a phone does not do as what it does,
	    so both refusals are linked from the phone set rather than living only
	    on the desktop screens that host them. */}
	<section class="ph-section">
		<Eyebrow size="sm" tone="muted">D-09 — FOUR PERMITTED, TWO REFUSED</Eyebrow>
		<ol class="ph-index">
			{PHONE_ROUTES.map((r) => (
				<li class="ph-index-row">
					<a class="ph-index-link" href={`${r.route}/`}>{r.id}</a>
					<span class="ph-index-what">{r.what}</span>
				</li>
			))}
			{REFUSALS.map((r) => (
				<li class="ph-index-row" data-refusal="true">
					<a class="ph-index-link" href={`${r.route}/`}>{r.id}</a>
					<span class="ph-index-what">{r.what}</span>
					<Badge tone="neutral">refused</Badge>
				</li>
			))}
		</ol>
		<Text size="sm" tone="muted">
			The two refusals are designed screens rather than missing ones. Neither ends in a dead
			end: the crop refusal leaves every other field on Home editable, and the case-study
			refusal leaves the project's card fields editable. That is the difference between an
			honest refusal and a broken screen.
		</Text>
	</section>
</Admin>

<style is:global>
	/* ═══ is:global IS REQUIRED HERE, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED ═══════════
	   Astro stamps its scoping attribute onto elements in the .astro template
	   and passes it as a PROP to a framework component — so a component with a
	   fixed prop list never receives it and a scoped rule cannot reach inside
	   the DOM it renders. This page carries AdminSidebar, and the sheet frame
	   below has to reach `.ds-atom-sheet`. Plan 13 recorded this as an ISLAND
	   rule; plan 15 measured that hydration is irrelevant — what matters is
	   that a framework component renders its own DOM.

	   App LAYOUT css only, per QUAL-03. Every colour is a charcoal token. */

	.ph-head,
	.ph-section,
	.ph-group {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	.ph-capability {
		margin: 0;
		padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
		border-left: 2px solid var(--wire);
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: var(--text-xs);
		letter-spacing: var(--ls-wide);
		color: var(--ink-2);
	}

	.ph-code {
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: var(--text-2xs);
		color: var(--ink-3);
	}

	.ph-groups {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-4);
	}
	.ph-group-head {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-2);
	}
	.ph-group-entity {
		font-size: var(--text-md);
		font-weight: 500;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	.ph-changes {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: var(--space-4);
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-2);
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		line-height: var(--lh-relaxed);
		color: var(--ink-2);
	}

	.ph-linkrow {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		margin: 0;
	}

	.ph-actions {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-3);
	}

	/* ── The 390 × 640 review frame ────────────────────────────────────────
	   The ONLY rules that touch a ds-atom class, and each one is a containment
	   override named in this file's header. */
	.ph-frame {
		position: relative;
		width: 100%;
		max-width: 390px;
		height: 640px;
		overflow: hidden;
		border: 1px solid var(--wire);
		border-radius: var(--radius-md);
		background: var(--page-bg);
	}
	.ph-frame-backdrop {
		position: absolute;
	}
	.ph-frame-sheet {
		position: absolute;
		height: 100%;
		width: 100%;
	}
	.ph-sheet-body {
		padding: 0;
	}

	/* ── The six-row D-09 list ─────────────────────────────────────────────── */
	.ph-index {
		list-style: none;
		margin: 0;
		padding: 0;
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-2);
	}
	.ph-index-row {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-3);
		padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
		border: 1px solid var(--rule);
		border-radius: var(--radius-md);
		background: var(--cream-2);
	}
	.ph-index-row[data-refusal="true"] {
		border-style: dashed;
	}
	.ph-index-link {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		min-height: 44px;
		font-family: var(--mono);
		font-size: var(--text-xs);
		color: var(--ink);
		text-decoration: none;
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--wire);
	}
	.ph-index-link:hover {
		color: var(--ochre-d);
		border-bottom-color: var(--ochre-d);
	}
	.ph-index-link:focus-visible {
		outline: 2px solid var(--focus);
		outline-offset: 2px;
	}
	.ph-index-what {
		font-size: var(--text-sm);
		color: var(--ink-2);
	}

	/* ══ THE 44px FLOOR ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
	   Last block in the file. These routes render at COMFORTABLE density, so
	   density-compact.css contributes nothing here and every target below is
	   the design system's own geometry or mine. Applied at every pointer,
	   not only coarse: a phone artefact reviewed on a desktop is still a phone
	   artefact, and the floor is the thing under review. */
	.ph-linkrow .ds-atom-link {
		min-height: 44px;
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
	}
</style>
